Principles
(6)
La Trobe seeks to attract, retain and develop very high quality staff to achieve the University’s strategic goals. As part of this commitment, it seeks to encourage, by way of promotion, those members of academic staff who demonstrate outstanding qualities in performing their responsibilities.
(7)
The University will recognise and reward sustained academic excellence through a transparent and consistent process of promotion. Promotion will be awarded based on evidence of the quality and impact of contributions to the University’s strategic goals at the academic level sought in each of the domains:
teaching
research and/or scholarship of learning and teaching as appropriate; and
service
(8)
Where an academic appointment specifically precludes or constrains contribution to one of these domains (for example, research-only positions), the application will be judged against the relevant domains of contribution.
(9)
Academic promotion at La Trobe University will be based on the following principles:
Criteria will be consistent with the criteria for selection and probationary review and the University’s
Performance Procedure - Annual Performance Cycle
, including La Trobe’s commitment to its cultural qualities and values;
Equality of opportunity forms the basis of conferring all forms of employment benefits at the University including promotion. The University values the diversity in its academic workforce;
Academic staff will be provided with appropriate advice and support in the preparation of applications and will receive clear and timely feedback on the outcomes;
Applications will be assessed on the evidence provided about the quality, outcomes and impact of contributions holistically across domains;
Application and assessment will be based on demonstrated performance, relative to opportunity. Applications from under-represented groups will be actively encouraged;
Evidence will be assessed over an academic staff member’s entire career, however more significance will be placed on achievements, outputs and impact in the period since last promotion or appointment to the current academic level;
Applications will be judged on their merit, there are no quotas on the number of staff promoted annually. In the case of a successful promotion outcome, it is the responsibility of the relevant academic unit to fund the salary costs. This also applies to staff holding research-only positions, whose salaries are usually provided by external funding bodies;
All staff involved in promotion processes will uphold the principles of confidentiality and privacy.

Part A - Domains of Academic Work
(10)
Applicants will demonstrate high levels of performance and achievement (relative to opportunity), appropriate to academic level, disciplinary field, type of appointment and agreed work plan, in the following three domains of academic work:
teaching
research and/or scholarship of learning and teaching as appropriate; and
service
(11)
It is expected that sustained performance and achievements are demonstrated at a level commensurate with the Minimum Standards for Academic Levels for the academic level sought.
(12)
For the purposes of this Policy, teaching activity includes but is not limited to:
creating and evaluating innovative and inclusive teaching, assessment and curriculum design strategies that are informed by research and supported by evidence of outcomes and impact on student learning;
developing effective, accessible and inclusive learning environments, student support and guidance;
effective adoption and evaluation of technologies to enhance the student learning experience;
assessing and giving feedback to students on their learning and learning support activities;
integration of scholarship, discipline research and professional learning activities;
demonstrated leadership in teaching, curriculum development, mentoring of teaching staff, and/or learning and teaching policy and strategy;
contribution to quality teaching culture;
securing internal or external grants to support instructional activities;
honours and graduate research supervison (can be addressed under teaching or research).
(13)
Research activity includes but is not limited to:
production of quality research publications and other outputs, including contract research reports, patents and creative works, with demonstrated indicators of esteem;
securing internal and external research funding;
demonstrated research impact and/or demonstrated research engagement;
research leadership and mentoring;
contribution to research culture;
honours and graduate research supervision (can be addressed under teaching or research);
effective development and management of partnerships with industry, government, business or community groups to support research and/or knowledge transfer.
(14)
Scholarship of learning and teaching which may be evidenced by:
educational research and scholarly activity, including journal publications and conference presentations;
securing teaching related research funding;
scholarly and innovative teaching practice and contribution to curriculum development;
contribution to professional bodies or communities of practice;
involvement in scholarly academic societies, editorial roles or peer review.
(15)
For further information, please refer to the
Academic Promotions Intranet
.
(16)
Service activity includes but is not limited to:
contribution to academic governance, leadership and University policy, which may include representation on committees/working groups and/or formal leadership positions at Course, Department, Research Centre, School, regional and/or University levels;
mentoring, supporting and managing the development and performance of other staff;
leadership and contribution to academic activities, including committees, forums, professional development, seminars, and networks;
contributions to the profession/discipline, including professional organisations within the discipline, journal reviewing and editorial functions;
engagement with industry, government, business and the broader community that aligns with La Trobe’s strategic goals and has demonstrable, sustained impact;
demonstrated high standards of professional and inclusive behaviour consistent with the University’s Cultural Qualities and commitment to equity, diversity and inclusion.
